I think your entire reply was sound except for one point: just because
slashdot had one article about Lojban 7 years doesn't make the
slashdot community aware of it.  The slashdot community is aware of
Linux.  That's the kind of aware we should shoot for with Lojban,
since Lojban is, after all, basically the linguistic equivilent of a
Linux or a Wikipedia at this point.

The point you made about awareness were minor, but still impressive,
all things considered.

The point you made about how it is being developed are well taken.

Finally, I was not aware that the growth was so significant.  It did
not come off that way to me as I investigated the community.

BTW, you asked me who the language is useful to.  I beleive that the
answer is the set of all human beings who speak imprecise languages.

As for Utopia and what it means in latin, why would you doom your own
charity project of the mind which you have invested a sizable
percentage of your life in by implying that there will never we a
community of Lojban speakers???

Bob, I do not mean you any disrespect whatsoever.  I am, in my own
way, trying to help.  I didn't just criticize.  I, for my own reasons,
am VERY interested in seeing this language spread and that is why I
brought this up.  I have things in mind to do in my own way to see
this goal come to fruition but I do still feel that this should be an
open topic and that this topic being discussion should not be
perceived as an afrontment to you.

I do not know Lojban yet, I only understand it's concepts and grammar,
so I cannot yet translate things into it.  But rest assured when I can
speak Lojban I intend to write and translate philosophy into it.  That
is my own vested interest.

Jared

